graph adjacency symmetry, shortest-path symmetry and zero diagonal, finite distances for the nominal connected graph, explicit infinite distances for a disconnected control, and triangle inequality on 500 deterministic sampled triples;
a synthetic exact-tie/duplicate \(k\)NN case with points \((0,0),(0,0),(1,0),(-1,0)\) verifies that no row selects itself, stable ties prefer lower indices, union symmetrization is exact, and retained edge weights equal the original distances exactly;
the reduced-separation generator has \(L_r=6+\pi(0.06)\), two length-3 branches, endpoint \((0.12,0)\), zero domain violation, and reused nominal quantiles to numerical precision;
PCA/ambient-MDS sample-projector equivalence and affine coordinate agreement;
ambient-MDS and Isomap training-point Gower extension replay;
uniform-circle affinity and Markov circulant discrepancies;
Markov row sums, stationarity \(\pi^\top P=\pi^\top\), detailed balance, \(L^2(\pi)\) eigenfunction Gram matrix, and direct-row versus spectral diffusion distance;
uniform-circle training-point Nyström replay;
hairpin graph-distance and circle two-mode-projector invariance under deterministic relabeling;
exact regeneration of hairpin splits/controls and circle grids;
hairpin truth poisoning leaves fits and raw extensions unchanged;
circle angle-truth poisoning changes truth while leaving observed train and midpoint points, fits, and raw Nyström extensions unchanged;
test-observation poisoning leaves the hairpin training fit unchanged;
anisotropic scaling changes at least one undirected neighbor edge;
graph diagnostic/metric records, including both edge semantics, and the \(L^2(\pi)\) circle angle/residual records agree exactly between CSV and JSON;
the exact CSV schema, finite values, nonempty labels, unique keys, artifact hashes, and byte-identical temporary regeneration.

These checks establish bounded numerical and leakage properties only.

training-mean scoring reference;
rank-one PCA;
ambient-distance classical MDS in one dimension;
union-\(k\) Isomap plus one-dimensional classical MDS; and
direct graph diagnostics without embedding.





raw Gaussian affinity and normalized Markov diagnostics;
full \(\alpha=1\) finite-operator diffusion distance;
first-two-nonconstant-mode Diffusion Maps at \(t=1\);
normalized Nyström midpoint extension; and
direct Fourier-subspace/operator diagnostics.

No method uses truth to choose \(k\), \(\varepsilon\), retained modes, graph edges, or extension parameters.

For observations generated from one scalar through a fixed curved map into six features:

does a one-coordinate nonlinear bottleneck improve held-out MSE over the training mean, rank-one PCA, and a trained linear autoencoder in the identical standardized metric;
does learning the encoder improve on a frozen random nonlinear encoder with a trained decoder;
are reconstruction, code activity, and checkpoint choice stable across five predeclared seeds;
how do results change for full-dimensional Gaussian data, independent high-variance nuisance features, few samples with a larger architecture, and shifted test support; and
which conclusions remain strictly about reconstruction rather than manifold structure, intrinsic dimension, unique coordinates, representation, or mechanism?

The verifier checks:

exact split regeneration for observations, \(s\), and noiseless curves;
test-observation poisoning leaves standardization, all candidates, checkpoint histories, and selected seeds unchanged;
poisoning every \(s\) and noiseless-truth array leaves all observation-only fits and reconstructions unchanged;
PCA and the analytic linear reconstruction are identical;
selected trained-linear-AE composite projector, subspace angle, and training MSE approach PCA within declared tolerances; failure would be labelled optimization failure;
centered finite-difference gradients for linear, nonlinear, and frozen-decoder networks agree with analytic backpropagation;
validation checkpoint losses and earliest-minimum epochs recompute exactly;
validation seed selection replays exactly;
repeated full-batch Adam training is deterministic;
frozen encoder parameters never change;
bottleneck shape is exactly \(n\times1\), parameter counts match architecture, and no skip path exists;
all losses, parameters, codes, reconstructions, and metric values are finite;
metric rows have the exact schema, nonempty labels, finite values, and unique composite keys; and
a clean temporary regeneration produces byte-identical generated artifacts.

These checks support implementation integrity, not manifold or mechanism claims.

training mean;
rank-one PCA;
trained rank-one linear AE;
fixed small nonlinear tanh AE;
frozen random tanh encoder with trained decoder; and
the larger nonlinear architecture in the few-sample stress.

No method receives \(s\), noiseless \(g(s)\), test preprocessing statistics, or test-selected epochs.

a model-free intrinsic dimension;
a smooth or injective manifold chart;
unique encoder/decoder parameters or code axes;
a probability model, posterior, or uncertainty distribution;
dynamics, temporal order, a vector field, or state transition;
a causal, biological, or physical mechanism;
universal nonlinear superiority over PCA; or
reliable extrapolation outside the tested range.

Let \(X\in\mathbb R^{n\times p}\) contain \(n\) selected observations as rows and \(p\) selected features as columns. A reproducible PCA analysis must declare:

which observations enter the fit and whether rows are independent units, trials, condition–time pairs, or another sampling unit;
equal sample weighting, as used throughout this object; a nonuniformly weighted variant changes the fitted mean, covariance, and objective and is outside the present scope;
sample-mean centering with \(\mu=n^{-1}\sum_i x_i\);
feature scaling, such as no rescaling, division by declared scale factors, or standardization to a correlation-matrix PCA;
exclusion rules and a complete analysis matrix; imputation or specialized missing-data PCA requires a separate method and validation argument; and
the target rank \(k\leq\min(n-1,p)\) for centered data, or the rule by which \(k\) will be selected.

Write \(D\) for a fixed nonsingular diagonal scaling matrix and let the transformed row vectors be \(z_i=D^{-1}(x_i-\mu)\). The matrix \(Z\) stacks the \(z_i^\top\). Changing samples, weighting policy, or \(D\) changes the problem being solved. This object always uses sample-mean centering; uncentered, origin-constrained PCA is a different variant outside its scope. In particular, covariance PCA is sensitive to feature units, whereas standardizing each feature produces correlation-matrix PCA (Jolliffe and Cadima 2016).

For a chosen rank \(k\), ordinary PCA returns:

a principal subspace \(\mathcal S_k\subseteq\mathbb R^p\);
an orthonormal basis \(V_k=[v_1,\ldots,v_k]\) for that subspace;
ordered eigenvalues \(\lambda_1\geq\cdots\geq\lambda_p\geq0\) measuring sample variance along the principal directions;
scores \(T=ZV_k\), which are coordinates of the transformed observations in the chosen basis; and
reconstructions \(\widehat Z=TV_k^\top=ZV_kV_k^\top\), mapped back to the original feature units by \(\widehat x_i=\mu+D\widehat z_i\).

Terminology is not uniform. Here principal directions, basis vectors, and loading vectors refer to the columns of \(V_k\). If “loadings” instead means variable–component correlations or the scaled vectors \(v_j\sqrt{\lambda_j}\), that convention must be stated rather than silently mixed with \(V_k\).

Ordinary PCA does not specify a probability distribution, latent prior, observation-noise model, or data-generating mechanism. Its operative assumptions are instead approximation choices:

a single global affine subspace is an appropriate summary after the declared centering and scaling;
Euclidean squared distance in the transformed coordinates is scientifically meaningful;
directions with larger sample variance deserve priority; and
an orthogonal rank-\(k\) projection is the permitted reconstruction class.

These choices can be poor when variance is dominated by units, artifacts, outliers, heteroscedastic noise, nuisance conditions, or scientifically irrelevant directions. Statistical uncertainty statements require additional sampling assumptions or resampling procedures; they do not follow from the PCA optimization alone.

Fixed-interval Kalman smoothing inherits all assumptions of the declared LGSSM and forward filter:

a Gaussian initial state;
linear transition and observation maps in the chosen coordinates;
Gaussian process and observation noises;
declared temporal independence and process/observation-noise independence;
fixed parameters throughout the interval;
a nonanticipating known input path with the timing used in the forward filter;
a valid first-order Markov state; and
non-informative missingness when absent observation rows are merely skipped.

Under these assumptions and exact arithmetic, the posterior path is jointly Gaussian and the RTS recursion gives exact marginal smoothing moments. Exactness is conditional on the model. It does not show that linearity, Gaussianity, the selected state dimension, \(A\), \(Q\), \(C\), \(R\), or the interval boundaries are scientifically correct.

Let \(p\) be the number of measured features or neurons, \(C\) the number of experimental conditions, and \(T_c\) the number of retained time samples for condition \(c\). For each condition, begin with an aligned matrix
\[
Y^{(c)}=
\begin{bmatrix}
y^{(c)}(t_1)&\cdots&y^{(c)}(t_{T_c})
\end{bmatrix}
\in\mathbb R^{p\times T_c}.
\]
The original reaching analysis used trial-averaged firing rates, neuron-wise soft normalization, event alignment, smoothing, and subtraction of each neuron’s across-condition mean at every time before PCA (Churchland et al. 2012). Those are consequential analysis choices, not universal requirements of the algebra. A reproducible jPCA analysis must declare:

the experimental unit, feature population, condition definitions, included trials, and whether neurons were simultaneous or assembled across recordings;
event alignment, time window, sampling interval, binning, smoothing, interpolation, and missing-sample handling;
whether trajectories are single-trial estimates or condition averages and how unequal trial counts are handled;
feature centering and scaling, including whether a time-varying across-condition mean is subtracted;
the PCA rank \(k\) and how it is selected;
the derivative estimator and boundary rule;
which conditions, trials, subjects, sessions, and time regions are assigned to training, validation, and test sets; and
the rule for ranking, orienting, displaying, and evaluating jPCA planes.

Stack the preprocessed matrices without creating transitions across condition boundaries:
\[
Y=\begin{bmatrix}Y^{(1)}&\cdots&Y^{(C)}\end{bmatrix}
\in\mathbb R^{p\times N},
\qquad
N=\sum_c T_c.
\]
Alignment can reduce timing variability and reveal repeatable structure, but it can also impose a shared temporal origin. Condition averaging suppresses trial variability and can make heterogeneous latency sequences appear smooth. Lebedev et al. showed that the across-condition subtraction used by the canonical pipeline materially changes which rotations appear, illustrating that preprocessing is part of the estimand rather than a neutral display operation (Lebedev et al. 2019).

For a chosen even or odd PCA rank \(k\), jPCA returns:

a PCA basis \(W_k\in\mathbb R^{p\times k}\) and reduced trajectories \(X^{(c)}=W_k^\top Y^{(c)}\);
an unconstrained least-squares state-to-derivative matrix \(\widehat M\in\mathbb R^{k\times k}\);
a skew-symmetric constrained matrix \(\widehat M_{\mathrm{skew}}=-\widehat M_{\mathrm{skew}}^\top\);
orthogonal two-dimensional invariant planes of \(\widehat M_{\mathrm{skew}}\), conventionally ordered by rotational rate \(|\omega_j|\);
projected condition trajectories in each selected plane; and
fit and descriptive diagnostics such as held-out derivative error, \(R^2\), plane occupancy variance, and state–derivative angle distributions.

These are deterministic summaries of the supplied, preprocessed trajectories. jPCA does not return a posterior distribution, observation-noise covariance, process-noise model, or latent-state uncertainty.

Canonical jPCA is not a generative probability model. Its operative assumptions are:

selected condition–time trajectories can be compared in one feature coordinate system;
preprocessing, alignment, smoothing, and averaging preserve the structure relevant to the question;
the retained PCA subspace contains the rotational tendency of interest;
a common linear relation \(\dot x\approx Mx\) is a useful local summary across the pooled conditions and sampled interval;
finite-difference or other derivative estimates are accurate enough for regression;
a skew-symmetric restriction is an informative definition of rotational structure; and
Euclidean orthogonality and squared derivative error in PCA coordinates are appropriate criteria.

No Gaussian latent-state distribution, Markov transition density, process noise, observation noise, Kalman inference, likelihood, or stationarity assumption is inherited from the related linear-Gaussian state-space model. LGSSM is only an adjacent temporal-model comparison. jPCA fits a deterministic relation to processed trajectories and does not separate latent evolution from a measurement model.
The equation \(\dot x=Mx\) is also an autonomous, homogeneous linear approximation. If unmodeled inputs, time-varying drive, condition-dependent forcing, feedback, offsets, or nonlinearities contribute materially, the fitted \(M\) can absorb their correlations with sampled states without identifying an autonomous law.

Let \(x_i\in\mathbb R^p\), \(i=1,\ldots,n\), denote selected complete observation vectors. A reproducible analysis must declare:

the observational unit and whether rows are independent units, repeated measurements, time samples, trials, subjects, or condition summaries;
the variables, units, transformations, exclusion rules, and missing-data policy;
whether the model is fitted to a covariance matrix, a correlation matrix after training-fitted standardization, or the raw observations through the equivalent Gaussian likelihood;
the candidate factor count \(q\) and its selection protocol; and
any loading zeros, equality constraints, rotations, or score conventions introduced after fitting.

The canonical formulation assumes independent and identically distributed observation vectors conditional on common parameters. Treating dependent repeated or temporal samples as independent changes the likelihood and its uncertainty interpretation.

For a chosen \(q\), maximum-likelihood FA estimates:

a mean \(\mu\in\mathbb R^p\);
a loading matrix \(\Lambda\in\mathbb R^{p\times q}\);
diagonal uniqueness covariance \(\Psi=\operatorname{diag}(\psi_1,\ldots,\psi_p)\) with \(\psi_j&gt;0\); and
the model covariance \(C=\Lambda\Lambda^\top+\Psi\).

For each observation it also defines a Gaussian posterior distribution over its latent factor vector. A reported “factor score” is therefore a chosen posterior summary or scoring rule, not an observed quantity and not the same object as a PCA projection score.

Fit preprocessing and parameters on training units only. Evaluation should include:

held-out negative log likelihood under \(\mathcal N(\widehat\mu,\widehat C)\), compared with \(q=0\), rank-matched PPCA, and declared covariance baselines;
residual covariance \(S_{\text{test}}-\widehat C\), especially reproducible off-diagonal structure that contradicts diagonal conditional residuals;
posterior predictive checks for marginal variances, correlations, tails, and subgroup or temporal residual patterns;
rank sensitivity and stability of the fitted common covariance or loading subspace across resamples, with orthogonal alignment before comparing loading representatives; and
convergence diagnostics, positivity of uniquenesses, multiple-start agreement, and sensitivity to scaling and exclusion rules.

Splits must preserve the true dependence unit: hold out entire subjects, sessions, trials, trajectories, or time blocks when observations within them are dependent. Factor recovery cannot be validated by visual separation alone. If known latent variables exist only in simulation, compare posterior estimates after an allowed alignment and separately score covariance recovery. Information criteria may supplement but should not replace held-out and misspecification checks.

Let \(N\) be the number of measured features or neurons, \(T\) the number of aligned time bins, and let \(a_1,\ldots,a_m\) denote declared categorical or discretized task variables. For a balanced full-factorial design, a trial-resolved tensor can be written
\[
\mathcal X\in
\mathbb R^{N\times T\times A_1\times\cdots\times A_m\times K},
\]
where \(K\) is the same number of trials in every condition cell. The fitted input in the bounded formulation here is the trial-averaged condition tensor
\[
\bar{\mathcal X}
\in
\mathbb R^{N\times T\times A_1\times\cdots\times A_m},
\]
flattened into a centered matrix \(X\in\mathbb R^{N\times M}\), with \(M=T\prod_j A_j\). Each condition–time column is given equal weight in the bounded formulation below. The trial-resolved tensor supplies independent repeats for estimating these means and for cross-validation; its individual trial columns do not replace \(X\) in the objective stated below.
A reproducible analysis must declare:

the experimental unit, trial inclusion rules and whether features were recorded simultaneously or assembled as a pseudo-population;
alignment, binning, smoothing, time warping and baseline subtraction;
feature scaling and centering;
the task variables, their coding, level order and which interactions are retained or grouped;
whether all factorial cells exist and how unequal trial counts are handled;
the ranks \(q_\phi\) allocated to each marginalization;
the regularization grid and cross-validation split unit; and
whether projections will be applied to condition means, single trials, or both.

Equal weighting of condition means answers a different question from weighting cells in proportion to their trial counts. Missing cells, correlated labels and unequal noise precision require an explicit model, weighting or rebalancing procedure rather than silent application of balanced formulas (Kobak et al. 2016).

The bounded 2016 dPCA formulation is a deterministic supervised approximation method, not a generative probability model. Its operative assumptions are:

declared task labels and their interactions provide scientifically relevant partitions of variation;
condition means can be compared in one common feature space;
linear readouts and linear reconstructions are adequate summaries;
squared Frobenius reconstruction loss and ridge regularization are appropriate criteria;
the factorial marginalization is meaningful under the declared balancing or weighting convention; and
enough independent trials exist to estimate condition means, regularization and held-out behavior.

Earlier dPCA work used an orthogonality-constrained probabilistic formulation, while the canonical reduced-rank formulation removed that constraint because task-related subspaces can be nonorthogonal (Brendel et al. 2011; Kobak et al. 2016). Neither formulation implies that the labeled effects are physical sources or that the data were generated by independent modules.

Calling \(f\) a vector field does not by itself imply that:

observed trajectories uniquely determine \(f\) away from, or even along, the sampled states;
a fitted local velocity field is the true physical or biological mechanism;
time-varying data or arrows drawn on a plot establish vector-field structure;
finite differences are exact instantaneous derivatives;
every state in \(\mathcal{X}\) has been observed;
a compatible trajectory exists uniquely or indefinitely from every state;
the field is autonomous, smooth, linear, low-dimensional, or noise-free;
a continuous-time vector field is the natural description of a discrete-time system; or
knowing the local rule automatically supplies its full finite-time evolution.

Calling a set a state space does not by itself imply that:

every admissible state has been observed or can be observed;
recorded samples cover the space densely or without bias;
every coordinate is a direct measurement;
the space is identical to a data matrix, point cloud, or plotted projection;
all coordinate combinations are admissible;
the representation is unique, minimal, low-dimensional, or Euclidean;
a particular distance, geometry, or neighborhood relation has been justified;
a rule of evolution, trajectory, or causal mechanism has been identified; or
the model-defined space is the complete physical state space of the real system.

Stability does not imply:

asymptotic convergence unless that stronger condition is shown;
resilience to parameter changes, inputs, noise, or model misspecification;
global behavior from all initial conditions unless global scope and forward support are proved;
a unique equilibrium or basin;
that a finite observed trajectory will visibly return; or
that low variance in data establishes dynamical stability;
that successful decoding, low reconstruction error, or a smooth posterior mean verifies all nearby initial states and all future times; or
that stability transfers through a non-injective observation or projection.

A limit cycle does not imply:

sinusoidal motion or a circular orbit;
attraction unless stability is specified;
that every nearby trajectory shares or converges to the same phase;
that a periodic-looking finite trace is generated autonomously;
that observed frequency alone identifies the state-space orbit;
absence of noise, inputs, transient dynamics, or multiple cycles;
that a spectral peak or autocorrelation peak identifies a closed state-space orbit;
that a closed projected curve is closed in the complete state space; or
that a periodic forced response persists without the forcing signal.

Calling \(x(t_0)=x_0\) an initial condition does not by itself imply that:

\(t_0\) is the time when the physical system began to exist;
\(x_0\) was directly or exactly measured;
the first data row is a complete state value;
the same nominal experimental condition produces the same \(x_0\) on every trial;
the evolution rule and parameters are known;
a trajectory exists for all later times or is unique;
changing \(x_0\) while changing parameters or inputs isolates an initial-condition effect; or
differences in later behavior are caused solely by differences in the initial condition.

Calling an evolution family a flow does not by itself imply that:

any arbitrary collection of trajectories comes from one shared flow;
similar-looking trajectories have identical vector fields or finite-time dynamics;
one observed trajectory determines the maps \(\phi_\tau\) for other initial states;
finite noisy observations identify a unique flow;
a visualization of curves establishes a dynamical mechanism;
the flow is defined for every state or for all positive and negative times;
every fixed-time map is a globally defined bijection \(\mathcal{X}\to\mathcal{X}\), even though a two-sided smooth local flow has reverse-time local inverses on corresponding supported domains;
every vector field automatically generates a unique global flow; or
an input-driven or explicitly time-dependent system has the same one-parameter flow structure.

An attractor does not imply:

that every initial state approaches it;
a unique attractor or basin;
a fixed point rather than a periodic or more complex set;
robustness to parameter changes or stochastic forcing;
that an empirical density peak is an attractor;
that finite trajectories identify the full basin;
that one trajectory approaching \(\Lambda\) establishes attraction from a neighborhood;
that recurrence, slow motion, clustering, decoding, low reconstruction error, or a density peak establishes invariance or transitivity;
that attraction implies Lyapunov stability or robustness to perturbations; or
that attraction explains the biological mechanism producing the data.

A fixed point does not imply:

stability, attraction, robustness, or biological desirability;
uniqueness of the fixed point;
that nearby trajectories are slow;
that a constant recording reveals a fixed latent state;
that the fixed point persists after parameters or inputs change;
that an estimated zero of a fitted vector field is a true system equilibrium;
that a small residual \(\|f(\widehat x)\|\) proves an exact root or an admissible state; or
that a non-injective observation preserves the number or identity of fixed states.

Input-driven dynamics do not imply:

that the input is known, measured without error, or experimentally controlled;
that state and input effects are identifiable from passive data;
linearity, time invariance, or additivity of input effects;
that an autonomous flow exists on the original state space;
that unexplained variation should be labeled input; or
that including an input proves a causal influence;
that the input is identifiable separately from state, parameters, or noise;
controllability, observability, or robustness;
that segment composition is an autonomous group or semigroup law; or
that arbitrary unknown inputs can be absorbed into a finite-dimensional state.

A latent variable does not imply:

that it is a true hidden mechanism or uniquely identifiable quantity;
low dimensionality, smooth geometry, or dynamical state;
that decoding or reconstruction validates its interpretation;
that different fitted coordinate systems represent different mechanisms;
causal influence on observations; or
that residual variation is meaningless noise.

Calling a set a phase space does not imply that:

plotted data have been shown to be the true state of the system;
the space is low-dimensional, Euclidean, smooth, or a manifold;
a two-dimensional visualization preserves the system’s dynamics;
temporal samples define a vector field or flow;
the chosen coordinates are unique or mechanistically privileged; or
nearby plotted points are close under a scientifically meaningful metric.
a phase portrait preserves elapsed time, density, uncertainty, or unobserved state variables; or
an invertible coordinate convention has been established for a learned projection.

Calling something a state does not by itself imply that:

it is directly observable or exactly recoverable from raw measurements;
a single measurement determines it uniquely;
it contains every physical detail of the real system;
it is the only valid state representation or uses unique coordinates;
it is minimal, low-dimensional, noise-free, or continuous;
its future is deterministic;
every component has an independent physical interpretation;
the proposed variables are actually adequate for prediction outside the assumptions and scale of the model; or
fitting or visualizing a time-varying feature vector establishes a dynamical mechanism.

A topology \(\mathcal T\) on a set \(X\) is a family of subsets such that:

\(\varnothing,X\in\mathcal T\);
arbitrary unions of members of \(\mathcal T\) belong to \(\mathcal T\);
finite intersections of members of \(\mathcal T\) belong to \(\mathcal T\).

The pair \((X,\mathcal T)\) is a topological space. A map is continuous when preimages of open sets are open. Sutherland gives these definitions and distinguishes topology induced by a metric (Sutherland 2024).

Calling a time-indexed object a trajectory does not by itself imply that:

its entries are states rather than observations or features;
the map represents an admissible system evolution merely because its values lie in \(\mathcal{X}\);
a governing equation or causal mechanism is known;
temporal correlation establishes dynamics;
the trajectory is continuous, smooth, noise-free, or fully observed;
interpolated points were actually visited;
the state description is complete or uniquely recoverable from data;
one trajectory determines the full set of possible evolutions;
two curves with the same plotted shape have the same timing; or
a low-dimensional projection preserves distinctions present in the original state description.

Let \(x_1,\ldots,x_n\in\mathbb R^p\) be observations. The data definition must state whether a row is a trial, subject, session, time point, condition average, trajectory window or another unit. Fit training preprocessing parameters only on the training split. With training mean \(\mu\) and a declared nonsingular diagonal scaling matrix \(S\), define
\[
u_i=S^{-1}(x_i-\mu).
\]
A reproducible bounded autoencoder must declare before test evaluation:

the training, validation and test split units;
missing-data and exclusion policies;
centering, scaling and any feature transforms;
bottleneck width \(d&lt;p\);
the exact finite encoder and decoder layer widths;
activation functions, output activation and the presence or absence of biases;
the absence of skip connections that bypass the bottleneck;
parameter initialization, optimizer, learning-rate schedule and batch size;
maximum epochs, validation metric, early-stopping rule and checkpoint selection;
weight decay or other permitted ordinary training regularization; and
the number of random initializations and model-selection rule.

The core loss is mean squared reconstruction error in the transformed coordinates. If features require another loss because of their support or scientific meaning, that defines another bounded variant and must be compared separately rather than called the same MSE baseline.

This autoencoder is not a probabilistic generative model. It specifies no prior \(p(z)\), observation likelihood \(p(x\mid z)\), posterior, observation-noise covariance or sampling procedure. Its assumptions are approximation and training choices:

the fixed network class contains a useful encoder–decoder composition;
MSE in the transformed feature coordinates reflects the reconstruction errors that matter;
the training and evaluation samples represent the intended deployment distributions;
the bottleneck and finite architecture constrain the fit enough to generalize;
the optimizer and initialization can find a useful solution; and
validation-based stopping and hyperparameter selection control overfitting adequately for the declared sample size.

A nonlinear autoencoder can fit a curved reconstruction map, but that fit is not evidence that the data-generating support is a smooth manifold or that the bottleneck is an intrinsic coordinate chart.

Let \(x_1,\ldots,x_n\) be observations and let \(\rho(x_i,x_j)\) be a declared dissimilarity, commonly a metric after fixed preprocessing. A reproducible analysis must specify:

the observational unit, selected sample, features, centering/scaling, missing-data treatment, and dependence structure;
the dissimilarity \(\rho\) and whether it is scientifically meaningful locally;
the affinity family and bandwidth convention;
whether the graph is fully connected, \(k\)-nearest-neighbor, mutual-neighbor, radius-truncated, or otherwise sparsified and symmetrized;
treatment of self-affinities, isolated vertices, duplicate observations, and connected components;
density-normalization exponent \(\alpha\);
diffusion time \(t\), retained coordinate count \(m\), and truncation rule; and
any out-of-sample extension and all parameters fitted from training data.

This object uses the Gaussian-style convention
\[
K_{ij}=\exp\!\left[-\frac{\rho(x_i,x_j)^2}{\varepsilon}\right],
\]
possibly followed by declared sparsification. Other conventions place \(2\sigma^2\) or \(4\varepsilon\) in the denominator, so a reported bandwidth value is meaningless without its formula.

Canonical Diffusion Maps are not a generative probability model for observations. Their operative assumptions and design commitments are:

the chosen dissimilarity and affinity encode useful local relationships;
graph connectivity at the selected bandwidth is scientifically meaningful rather than an artifact of units, nuisance variables, or sampling gaps;
the symmetric nonnegative affinity supports a reversible Markov construction;
finite-sample spectral modes are stable enough to summarize the target relationships; and
when a continuum or manifold interpretation is invoked, additional smoothness, sampling, boundary, bandwidth, and asymptotic conditions hold.

The last point is optional and demanding. The finite graph algorithm can be computed without assuming a manifold. Conversely, successful computation or an appealing embedding does not verify the assumptions needed for a Laplace–Beltrami limit.

Evaluation must match the intended use and include graph diagnostics before downstream scores:

report connectivity, isolated vertices, degree/stationary-mass distributions, leading eigenvalues, eigengaps, and sensitivity to bandwidth, graph rule, \(\alpha\), \(t\), and \(m\);
quantify the truncated-coordinate error relative to the full or higher-rank diffusion distance on the fitted graph;
compare eigenspaces across resamples or folds using principal angles rather than unaligned eigenvectors when signs or multiplicities are ambiguous;
compare with PCA and declared spectral/graph baselines at matched dimension and without test-set graph leakage;
if new-point deployment is intended, evaluate a training-fitted extension on held-out dependence units and compare it with a full refit after alignment; and
when synthetic ground truth is available, separately assess operator/eigenspace recovery, diffusion-distance recovery, neighborhood behavior, and downstream task performance.

No universal reconstruction objective is part of Diffusion Maps. Classification, regression, clustering, or visualization scores are application-specific and must be predeclared. Subjects, sessions, trajectories, or trials should be split as whole units when within-unit observations are dependent.
For a fixed training operator, a Nyström/eigenfunction extension can evaluate a new point by its normalized affinities to training points and divide by the relevant retained nonzero eigenvalue. A zero mode is not defined by that formula; very small nonzero modes are unstable. The extension is approximate, can also fail for poorly connected new points, and need not equal recomputing the graph and spectrum with the point included (Bengio et al. 2003).

A future non-executable toy contract would sample points at known angles on a circle embedded in \(\mathbb R^2\), with both uniform and deliberately nonuniform angular densities. It would:

construct Gaussian affinities over a bandwidth grid with explicitly retained self-loops;
compare \(\alpha=0\) and \(\alpha=1\), several integer diffusion times, and rank-two coordinates;
compare with rank-two PCA and unscaled normalized-Laplacian coordinates;
align the first nontrivial two-dimensional eigenspace to the known span of \(\sin\theta\) and \(\cos\theta\), recognizing that the repeated continuum eigenvalue permits arbitrary rotation;
score graph connectivity, operator/eigenspace error, diffusion-distance truncation error, resampling stability, and held-out Nyström error; and
add negative controls consisting of a disconnected two-arc sample, a bandwidth that nearly completes the graph, and an asymmetric affinity requiring a nonreversible treatment.

The contract would test how density, bandwidth, connectivity, spectral multiplicity, and extension error affect recovery. It would not treat a circular visualization as proof of topology, manifold structure, or intrinsic dimension. No data, code, result, or benchmark is produced in this Method Object.

For trial \(n=1,\ldots,N\), let
\[
Y^{(n)}=
\begin{bmatrix}
y^{(n)}_1&\cdots&y^{(n)}_{T_n}
\end{bmatrix}
\in\mathbb R^{p\times T_n},
\]
where \(p\) is the number of observed channels and \(t^{(n)}_1,\ldots,t^{(n)}_{T_n}\) is that trial’s declared time grid. In the canonical neural formulation, \(y^{(n)}_t\) contains nonoverlapping binned spike counts after a square-root transformation; unlike a two-stage smoother followed by static dimensionality reduction, the transformed counts are not presmoothed before GPFA (Yu et al. 2009).
A reproducible analysis must declare:

the experimental unit, recording population, simultaneous-versus-pseudo-population status, inclusion rules, and trial/sequence boundaries;
bin width, time grid, event alignment, analyzed interval, count transformation, and handling of unequal trial lengths;
whether an across-condition or trial-specific mean has been removed, represented in \(d\), or modeled separately;
candidate latent dimension \(q\) and its selection rule;
covariance-kernel family, signal variance convention, timescale constraints, and any latent nugget/noise term;
observation covariance structure and whether diagonal \(R\) is scientifically defensible;
parameter initialization, optimization, restarts, convergence checks, and regularization;
missing-observation and censoring rules; and
training, validation, and test split units.

The canonical multi-trial model treats trials as conditionally independent given shared parameters. It does not require equal \(T_n\), but it does assume that all trials use the same loading matrix, observation offset, residual covariance, and kernel-hyperparameter family. Alignment resets within-trial time and does not create continuity between trial endpoints.

For a selected latent dimension \(q\), fitted GPFA returns:

a loading matrix \(C\in\mathbb R^{p\times q}\);
an observation offset \(d\in\mathbb R^p\);
a diagonal observation-residual covariance \(R\in\mathbb R^{p\times p}\);
one covariance kernel and fitted hyperparameters for each latent coordinate;
a marginal Gaussian distribution over every observed trial;
a joint Gaussian posterior over the complete latent path for each trial; and
posterior means, marginal covariances, cross-time covariances, samples, or posterior predictions derived from that path distribution.

The posterior mean is one trajectory summary. It is not the latent trajectory random element itself, a measured state path, or a trajectory with parameter uncertainty integrated out. If parameters are estimated by maximum likelihood and then held fixed, reported posterior covariance is conditional on those plug-in parameters.

A future non-executable analytic/generation contract should use \(q=2\) independent latent GPs observed through \(p=8\) channels over multiple independent trials. Let
\[
k_1(t,t')=\exp\!\left[-\frac{(t-t')^2}{2(0.08)^2}\right],
\qquad
k_2(t,t')=0.95\exp\!\left[-\frac{(t-t')^2}{2(0.30)^2}\right]+0.05\delta_{t,t'}.
\]
Choose a full-column-rank loading matrix \(C\), offset \(d\), and heterogeneous diagonal
\[
R=\operatorname{diag}(r_1,\ldots,r_8).
\]
For each trial, draw new GP latent values on a declared regular or irregular grid, generate Gaussian observations, and retain latent truth. Some test trials should contain masked channels and time blocks.
Compare:

mean plus diagonal Gaussian;
static two-factor FA;
cross-validated two-stage smoothing plus FA;
correctly specified GPFA;
a GPFA model with one wrong common timescale; and
a two-state LGSSM fitted to the same sequences.

Score held-out marginal likelihood, masked-channel/time predictive density, observable covariance, loading-subspace error, aligned posterior-mean error, posterior coverage, and timescale recovery. Required stress cases are:

an abrupt changepoint that violates squared-exponential smoothness;
correlated observation residuals violating diagonal \(R\);
low-rate Poisson counts analyzed with the Gaussian square-root approximation;
one latent coordinate with nearly independent temporal values;
two equal-timescale coordinates exposing rotational ambiguity;
event-time jitter and incorrect trial alignment;
informative missingness; and
a condition-locked mean omitted from \(d\), forcing latent factors to absorb deterministic trial structure.

Recovery claims must respect sign, permutation, scale, and identical-kernel rotational equivalences. The toy contract would distinguish posterior inference under a known model from parameter learning and model selection. No code, data, figure, or result is produced in this Method Object.

Let \(x_1,\ldots,x_n\in\mathbb R^p\) be the fitted observations after a declared feature construction and preprocessing pipeline. A reproducible analysis must specify:

the observational unit represented by each row and any dependence among rows;
centering, feature scaling, exclusion, missing-data and duplicate-point policies;
the ambient dissimilarity \(\delta(x_i,x_j)\) used both to choose neighbors and, unless explicitly separated, to weight graph edges;
either a \(k\)-nearest-neighbor rule or an \(\varepsilon\)-radius rule;
directed versus symmetrized \(k\)-nearest-neighbor construction, including whether mutual or union symmetrization is used;
tie breaking, zero-distance and duplicate handling;
the treatment of disconnected components;
the target coordinate dimension \(d\) or the training-only rule used to choose it; and
whether new observations will require an explicitly declared out-of-sample extension.

Ordinary Isomap commonly uses Euclidean distance after preprocessing, but that choice is not automatic. Feature scaling can alter neighbor identities, edge lengths, graph connectivity, shortest paths and the final embedding.

For the fitted observations, Isomap returns:

a neighborhood graph \(G=(V,E)\) whose vertices correspond to observations;
weighted shortest-path distances \(d_G(i,j)\) within the analyzed connected component or components;
a double-centered matrix derived from squared graph distances;
eigenvalues diagnosing how nearly those graph distances admit the requested Euclidean representation; and
centered coordinates \(y_i\in\mathbb R^d\) for the fitted observations.

Standard Isomap does not return a generative distribution, a posterior over coordinates, a unique inverse map from coordinates to observations, or automatically valid coordinates for observations not included in the fitted graph.

Isomap is not a probabilistic generative model. Its interpretation nevertheless relies on strong approximation assumptions:

nearby ambient observations are close along the candidate structure rather than merely close because two distant branches approach each other;
sampling is dense enough that graph paths can follow relevant paths without large gaps;
neighborhood scale is large enough for connectivity but small enough to avoid shortcut edges;
observation noise and outliers do not substantially corrupt neighbor identities;
the relevant connected region is represented by the fitted sample; and
the resulting path metric is sufficiently close to a Euclidean distance matrix for classical MDS to represent it at dimension \(d\).

Bernstein et al. prove graph-distance approximation under sufficient conditions that include geodesic convexity (so the relevant minimizing geodesics exist), fine enough sample coverage, and edge scales controlled relative to curvature radius and separation between distinct branches. These are conditional theorem hypotheses, not tests that a finite dataset satisfies the assumptions (Bernstein et al. 2000). The original Isomap result additionally emphasizes a favorable case in which intrinsic geometry is isometric to a convex region of Euclidean space; curved spaces or non-Euclidean path metrics need not flatten globally without distortion (Tenenbaum et al. 2000).

The recursion inherits every assumption of the LGSSM. In particular:

state evolution and observation are linear in the chosen coordinates;
the prior and noises are Gaussian;
process and observation noises are white, mutually independent, and independent of the initial state;
the Markov state and conditional-observation structure is correctly specified;
matrices and covariance parameters used by the filter are fixed during the recursion;
the input path is nonanticipating under the declared within-step timing; and
any missing-observation pattern is treated as observed/fixed and non-informative unless a missingness model is added.

Under these conditions and exact arithmetic, the recursion gives the exact Gaussian filtering distributions. Kalman’s original state-space treatment derives optimal linear estimates through orthogonal projection and explicitly distinguishes filtering, prediction, and smoothing by the estimated time relative to the latest conditioning observation (Kalman 1960). This object uses the narrower Bayesian linear-Gaussian interpretation: exactness is conditional on the stated probability model, not evidence that the model generated the scientific system.

For one sequence of length \(T\), let

\(x_t\in\mathbb R^p\) be the observed vector;
\(u_t\in\mathbb R^r\) be an optional known input or regressor; and
\(z_t\in\mathbb R^q\) be the latent state.

A reproducible analysis must declare:

the observational unit, sequence boundaries, sampling interval, ordering, and whether matrices are assumed common across sequences;
feature definitions, units, transformations, exclusions, and missing-data policy;
which inputs are fixed or externally supplied at fitting and prediction time, and when each value becomes available;
latent dimension \(q\) and its selection protocol;
whether parameters are fixed from external knowledge or learned from training sequences; and
the exact target: forecasting, filtering, smoothing, marginal likelihood, parameter learning, or simulation.

Sequences, subjects, sessions, or trials are not interchangeable replicates. Their dependence and parameter sharing must be stated.

The model specification is a parameter tuple
\[
\theta=(m_1,P_1,A,B,C,D,Q,R),
\]
with dimensions conforming to the state, input, and observation spaces. Conditional on \(\theta\), it defines:

the joint Gaussian law \(p_\theta(z_{1:T},x_{1:T}\mid u_{1:T})\);
predictive distributions for future states and observations;
filtering distributions conditioned only on observations available through the target time;
smoothing distributions conditioned on a complete declared observation interval;
the marginal sequence likelihood after integrating out \(z_{1:T}\); and
model-conditional latent-state summaries with uncertainty.

An implementation may additionally return fitted parameters, forecast means/covariances, filtered or smoothed state moments, innovations, and likelihood contributions. These outputs have different conditioning information and must be labeled accordingly.

Evaluation must respect sequence order and the information set of the intended task:

split entire subjects, sessions, trials, or trajectories when dependence crosses adjacent time points;
fit preprocessing, dimension selection, parameters, and regularization on training sequences only;
for forecasting, report one- and multi-step predictive log likelihood, error, and interval calibration using no future observations beyond the forecast origin;
for filtering, evaluate \(p(z_t\mid x_{1:t})\) or observation predictions using only online information;
for smoothing, label the task retrospective and permit \(x_{t+1:T}\) only within that separately evaluated target;
report held-out marginal sequence likelihood and compare with static and observation-space temporal baselines;
inspect innovation means, covariance, autocorrelation, temporal residual structure, and sensitivity to state dimension and covariance constraints; and
under synthetic known truth, assess state and parameter recovery only after an allowed similarity alignment, while separately scoring invariant predictions and observable covariance.

Random point-wise train/test splits are generally invalid for sequence forecasting. Comparing a smoother with an online filter on the same target without acknowledging future information is also invalid. High likelihood or low state error under the assumed model does not establish that the latent coordinates are unique or mechanistic.
Missing observations can be handled by evaluating each observation update on the observed components—equivalently selecting the relevant rows and covariance submatrices—provided the missingness mechanism is ignorable under the declared analysis. Informative missingness, censoring, unknown inputs, or observation-time selection require their own model. These variants do not change the need to keep future information out of online evaluation.

Treating the equation as a curve rather than a constraint. The expression \(\dot{x}=f(x)\) specifies which differentiable curves qualify as solutions; it is not itself one trajectory.
Equating existence with uniqueness. A continuous field can admit solutions without selecting exactly one solution from an initial state.
Equating local with global. Local Lipschitz continuity gives local existence and uniqueness and hence a unique maximal continuation, not an all-time solution.
Ignoring the declared domain. A solution must remain in \(\mathcal{X}\). Reaching its boundary can end the maximal solution even when the coordinate values stay bounded.
Using \(t\) and \(\tau\) interchangeably. Clock time labels a trajectory relative to \(t_0\); elapsed time indexes autonomous evolution maps.
Calling one trajectory a flow. One fixed \(x_0\) gives one trajectory. A flow organizes evolution across multiple initial states and must satisfy identity and composition where defined.
Assuming every vector field has a global flow. Even a smooth vector field can have finite-time blow-up.
Inferring a field from a display. A plotted curve or fitted arrow field is not by itself evidence for a unique autonomous mechanism.

This formalism also does not imply that:

every observed time series is a dynamical-system trajectory;
every time-varying latent representation admits a valid autonomous vector field;
one observed trajectory uniquely determines a vector field, either away from or along incompletely sampled states;
multiple similar trajectories prove one shared flow;
predictive success establishes mechanistic correctness;
an inferred latent state is the uniquely true physical state;
all systems are autonomous or continuous-time;
all scientifically relevant state spaces are Euclidean; or
all vector fields generate global flows.

For multiple independent trials generated by two latent Gaussian processes with different timescales and observed through one known factor-analysis layer:

does an exact known-parameter GPFA posterior recover latent paths and calibrated uncertainty;
can a finite train-plus-validation timescale grid recover a useful plug-in prior without test or latent-truth leakage;
how much do temporal coupling, cross-channel sharing, and the observation layer contribute to masked prediction;
how does a one-lag-matched LGSSM/RTS approximation differ from the non-Markov squared-exponential prior; and
how do changepoints, residual correlation, low counts, equal kernels, alignment error, informative missingness, and wrong covariance families break the interpretation?

A smooth posterior mean is a conditional expectation under a selected covariance prior and observation model. It is retrospective, parameter-conditional, and not a discovered transition equation, vector field, intrinsic trajectory, or mechanism (Yu et al. 2009; Cunningham and Yu 2014).

For each split and trial:

independently draw each latent path from its finite-grid GP covariance;
independently draw Gaussian observation residuals;
generate the complete \(24\times6\) observation array;
freeze the complete array and latent truth;
create evaluation masks as separate views of selected test trials; and
expose hidden observations and latent truth only to final scoring and implementation oracles.

The master seed is 20260819. Random streams use Generator(PCG64(SeedSequence(words))) with explicit stage, split, trial, latent, residual, mask, stress, and baseline codes stored in the manifest.
Generation, observation-layer fitting, timescale selection, posterior inference, masking, alignment, and scoring are separate functions. Parameter selection receives complete train and validation observations, but no test values, test masks, or latent arrays.

The known observation layer \(C,d,R\) is frozen. The selected-timescale GPFA baseline evaluates the nine ordered pairs
\[
\tau_1\in\{0.06,0.08,0.14\},
\qquad
\tau_2\in\{0.18,0.30,0.45\}.
\]
The true pair is included. For each pair, compute total train-plus-validation observation marginal negative log likelihood under independent trials. Select the minimum total NLL; break exact ties lexicographically by \((\tau_1,\tau_2)\). Test observations and every latent truth array are excluded.
This is a finite plug-in covariance-prior selection baseline. It is not unrestricted maximum-likelihood GPFA, does not learn \(C,d,R\), and does not integrate hyperparameter uncertainty. Rasmussen and Williams describe marginal-likelihood hyperparameter learning and its possible ridges or local alternatives; this finite grid deliberately avoids claiming a general optimizer (Rasmussen and Williams 2006, ch. 5).




wrong common squared-exponential timescale: \((0.14,0.14)\);
static FA latent dimension: two;
six deterministic starts for bounded static FA;
FA uniqueness floor: \(10^{-6}\);
FA iteration cap: 300 and relative likelihood tolerance \(10^{-9}\);
covariance symmetry tolerance: \(10^{-10}\);
covariance PSD tolerance: \(10^{-9}\);
posterior/direct-conditioning discrepancy tolerance: \(10^{-9}\);
pointwise interval multiplier: 1.959963984540054; and
deterministic byte comparison for every artifact.

The single summary.png contains four bounded panels:

one masked test trial showing true latent paths, known-parameter and selected-tau posterior means, and conditional 95% intervals;
masked-channel and masked-block predictive NLL for known, selected, and wrong-common-timescale GPFA;
negative-condition outcomes and informative-missingness warnings; and
selected timescale, covariance/posterior oracles, poison checks, and interpretation limits.

The caption must state that all posterior intervals condition on fixed parameters, use the full retained trial retrospectively, and omit parameter-selection uncertainty. Smooth curves are not evidence of learned dynamics or mechanism.

For a scalar discrete-time linear-Gaussian state-space model (LGSSM) with retained latent ground truth:

do a known-parameter Kalman filter, a known-parameter fixed-interval Rauch–Tung–Striebel (RTS) smoother, and direct batch Gaussian conditioning return the same model-conditional posterior marginals under their respective information sets;
how do online prediction, online filtering, and retrospective smoothing differ from latent recovery and from observation prediction;
how much performance and calibration change when parameters are selected from a finite grid rather than supplied by the generator; and
how do wrong noise ratios, a contiguous missing block, a false sequence boundary, near-unit-root dynamics, and informative missingness expose model or interpretation failures?

The primary acceptance question is numerical and inferential correctness under the declared model. It is not whether the recovered mean merely looks smooth.

Exactly one composite summary.png is allowed. It must have a scientific checking purpose and contain no decorative panels:

one predeclared test sequence with latent truth, observations, filter mean, smoother mean, and filter 95% model-conditional intervals;
the same sequence with the missing block visibly marked;
numerical oracle and leakage summaries; and
compact metric and failure-diagnostic panels distinguishing known parameters, selected parameters, wrong-(q/r), reset, near-unit-root, and informative-missingness stresses.

The caption must state that the smoother is retrospective and that the intervals condition on fixed parameters. Visual overlap, smoothness, or a compelling trajectory is never an acceptance oracle.

For observations generated by a known rank-two Gaussian factor model:

does ordinary PCA recover the leading eigenspace of the observable covariance;
does correctly specified diagonal-noise FA recover the common loading subspace, common covariance, diagonal uniquenesses, and observable covariance up to loading rotations;
how does isotropic-noise PPCA differ from heterogeneous-noise FA; and
